Connect projections and interfaces to a declared reference frame (@engineering-geometric-representations: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/practice/Technical-drawings.lingua#r_3PEBG59JH06QGKQCR5YAKKF3R2") { r_0475C7C1DBB15BA404CFEA8D83
An engineering representation answers a particular question. A perspective sketch can make an assembly recognizable. A geometrical view expresses selected positions and sizes. A schematic expresses connections, often without physical scale. A requirements table names permitted behavior. None automatically supplies the information carried by all the others. Begin with [Turn an intended use into requirements and observable evidence](Turn-an-intended-use-into-requirements-and-observable-evidence.lingua#r_0C3988C75ECA342B8BFC943458) and [Trace material energy and information across interfaces](Trace-material-energy-and-information-across-interfaces.lingua#r_0247145FE15239D16A42C0B328): decide which relation needs to be communicated before choosing its representation.

Consider a hypothetical open teaching tray with two rectangular inserts arranged end to end. Use millimetres, let x increase along the tray, y across its width and z upward, and put x=0 at the left inner reference plane. All dimensions refer to the same stipulated 20 °C, unloaded configuration. At the nominal configuration, the right inner reference plane is x=50. Insert B occupies x from 0 to 20, insert C from 20 to 49, leaving a 1 mm longitudinal gap. The ideal insert cross-section is 12 mm in y by 5 mm in z. The tray's outer walls, lateral fits, strength and surface form are not specified by this longitudinal model. The example supplies no construction or service-performance claim.

Reuse [Distinguish a point a displacement and its coordinates](../../mathematics/core/Distinguish-a-point-a-displacement-and-its-coordinates.lingua#r_062423188E960EBAB9A92CE3E4) and [Resolve a vector into a projection and an orthogonal remainder](../../mathematics/core/Resolve-a-vector-into-a-projection-and-an-orthogonal-remainder.lingua#r_0E1019900E901787E7D6D8001B). Define three coordinate views explicitly: XY retains (x,y), XZ retains (x,z), and YZ retains (y,z). Each forgets one coordinate. For P=(20,3,5) mm, they show (20,3), (20,5) and (3,5) mm respectively. Q=(20,9,5) mm has the same XZ projection as P, so one view cannot distinguish them. Corresponding points in suitably identified views can recover missing coordinates; unrelated silhouettes do not necessarily determine a unique object. Views also need visible/hidden-feature and section information for internal geometry.

A change of reference frame changes numbers without changing the ideal object. For the rotation and translation (u,v,w)=(100−y,20+x,5+z), the x direction becomes the positive v direction. P becomes (97,40,10) mm. Its inverse is (x,y,z)=(v−20,100−u,w−5). The right gap still has length 1 mm, now along v; subtracting u coordinates would ask about a different direction. This is a rigid coordinate transformation, not a physical enlargement. Reuse [Read a matrix as a map and distinguish translation from linearity](../../mathematics/core/Read-a-matrix-as-a-map-and-distinguish-translation-from-linearity.lingua#r_03CC45432746F4DD0737B769A4) to distinguish it from scaling a drawing or changing an actual part.

**Read and draw:** the shared drawing lesson next supplies line, view and section vocabulary. Consult [Desenho Técnico — Etchepare and Gravina](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Desenho-Tecnico-Etchepare-Gravina.lingua#r_4MDHAHPVZTA83F05M2NK2YR5PT)'s projection page and [Engineering drawings for measurement — NPL Guide 79](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Engineering-Drawings-for-Measurement-NPL.lingua#r_48E0H38P8GEB0XPX68MYXGGA48)'s coordinate discussion, with their inspection limits. Draw the same tray in the three explicitly named coordinate planes. Label axes and units rather than implying a standardized first-/third-angle layout that has not been declared. Show which dimension each view hides. A section is an imagined cut for representation; it does not mean removing that material from the intended object.

**Transfer:** a water-system schematic must preserve flow connections, an electrical schematic connection identity, and a software diagram information interfaces. Their drawn line lengths generally do not supply pipe lengths, wire resistance or message delay. State the additional geometrical or physical model needed instead of deriving performance from the appearance of the diagram.

**Continue:** [Technical drawings — turn views into a spatial description](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/practice/Technical-drawings.lingua#r_3PEBG59JH06QGKQCR5YAKKF3R2).

**Return:** [Engineering](../Engineering.lingua#r_5DA6VY09MAD2N8D9SH0FFJVV68).
